WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.066 --> 00:00:10.830
Turkey's largest city, Istanbul, is the only city in the world to straddle two continents—the western side of

2
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:15.873
Istanbul is in Europe, while the eastern side of the city is in Asia.

3
00:00:16.693 --> 00:00:22.396
One of the most fascinating places in the world, Istanbul has been the capital of three empires and

4
00:00:22.436 --> 00:00:27.439
been known by many names, including Byzantium, Constantinople, and Istanbul.

5
00:00:28.139 --> 00:00:35.369
Its rich history, beautiful setting, and elegant skyline of domes and minarets make Istanbul an exotic crossroads of

6
00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.431
art, religions, and cultures.

7
00:00:38.212 --> 00:00:40.694
Flowing through the heart of the city is the Bosporus Strait.

8
00:00:41.375 --> 00:00:46.219
This waterway, linking the Black Sea with the Sea of Marmara, has been one of the world's most

9
00:00:46.319 --> 00:00:49.762
important channels for trade and cultural exchange for thousands of years.

10
00:00:50.723 --> 00:00:55.187
A group of Greeks built the city of Chalcedon on the Asian side of the Bosporus in the

11
00:00:55.227 --> 00:00:56.548
seventh century BC.

12
00:00:57.164 --> 00:01:02.806
These settlers were ridiculed as being "blind" for choosing a site so geographically unprotected.

13
00:01:03.846 --> 00:01:09.387
Legend has it that the oracle of Apollo at Delphi told the leader of another group of settlers,

14
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:14.109
a man named Byzas, to found a city opposite the "land of the blind."

15
00:01:14.969 --> 00:01:20.550
Soon, these Greeks settled on the higher and more protected European side of the Bosporus, where they could

16
00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:23.391
take advantage of the Golden Horn, a natural harbor.

17
00:01:24.091 --> 00:01:27.653
Eventually, both sides became one city, known as Byzantium.

18
00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:35.878
The area existed as an independent Greek state until around 195 AD, when the Roman Emperor Septimius Severus

19
00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:38.260
besieged the city for two years.

20
00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.083
He was angry with the people of Byzantium for supporting one of his enemies in a civil war.

21
00:01:43.684 --> 00:01:47.666
The city's residents starved within its walls, and many buildings were destroyed.

22
00:01:48.407 --> 00:01:54.831
After Byzantium finally surrendered, the emperor destroyed its walls as punishment, only to rebuild the city in the

23
00:01:54.891 --> 00:02:01.927
Roman style, with new walls and an arena for horse racing known as a hippodrome—the largest ever built

24
00:02:02.048 --> 00:02:03.068
in ancient times.

25
00:02:03.949 --> 00:02:10.653
Constantine the Great became the Roman emperor in 324 AD, and six years later, proclaimed Byzantium the official

26
00:02:10.713 --> 00:02:11.833
capital of the empire.

27
00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:14.735
The city became known as Constantinople.

28
00:02:15.816 --> 00:02:22.880
A convert to Christianity himself—the first Roman emperor to embrace this faith—Constantine built many beautiful churches.

29
00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.501
He made the city a place where Christians could practice their religion without persecution.

30
00:02:28.141 --> 00:02:33.903
He made it the jewel of the empire with projects such as public baths, meeting halls, covered streets,

31
00:02:34.203 --> 00:02:39.404
and beautiful plazas filled with works of art from all over the territories under his control.

32
00:02:40.165 --> 00:02:47.107
When the Roman Empire was officially split into eastern and western halves in 395 AD, Constantinople became the

33
00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:52.048
capital of the Eastern Roman Empire, what historians now call the Byzantine Empire.

34
00:02:53.216 --> 00:03:00.122
When Justinian became the Byzantine emperor in 527 AD, he started to rebuild the Hagia Sophia—"The Church of

35
00:03:00.202 --> 00:03:04.485
Holy Wisdom"—which had been destroyed by riots twice in the previous two centuries.

36
00:03:05.346 --> 00:03:12.352
After it was completed, Justinian referred to King Solomon's magnificent temple in Jerusalem and exclaimed: "Solomon, I have

37
00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:13.092
outdone you!"

38
00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:20.318
The center of international trade, with a population over 300,000, the city was now a place where artists

39
00:03:20.398 --> 00:03:24.541
flourished, creating beautiful mosaics, religious icons, and silks.

40
00:03:25.522 --> 00:03:29.384
In 542, however, bubonic plague hit the famed city.

41
00:03:30.044 --> 00:03:36.488
Transmitted by infected fleas, the plague caused swellings on the body, a high fever, and death for between

42
00:03:36.568 --> 00:03:39.430
40 and 50 percent of Constantinople's population.

43
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:45.433
Gravesites in the city filled up quickly, and corpses were thrown into the streets and sea.

44
00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:48.695
The plague led to a period of decline for the city.

45
00:03:49.155 --> 00:03:53.502
Even in the eighth century, only fifty thousand people still lived in Constantinople.

46
00:03:54.283 --> 00:03:59.107
During the Crusades of the Middle Ages, Constantinople suffered another, even greater devastation.

47
00:03:59.768 --> 00:04:05.192
The Crusades were a series of religious wars between European Christians and Muslims for control of the holy

48
00:04:05.252 --> 00:04:07.534
city of Jerusalem and surrounding lands.

49
00:04:08.294 --> 00:04:14.719
In 1204, a young Byzantine convinced the army of the Fourth Crusade to attack Constantinople despite the fact

50
00:04:14.759 --> 00:04:15.840
that the city was Christian.

51
00:04:16.481 --> 00:04:20.104
He promised to pay the crusaders handsomely if they could place him on the throne.

52
00:04:20.748 --> 00:04:26.692
He never paid them, and the crusaders took their revenge on the city—sacking it, burning books, and destroying

53
00:04:26.752 --> 00:04:28.694
priceless works of art and holy relics.

54
00:04:29.514 --> 00:04:34.418
Residents fled in terror as the soldiers rampaged through the streets, looting and burning.

55
00:04:35.158 --> 00:04:39.341
Even the sacred Hagia Sophia was desecrated and stripped of everything of value.

56
00:04:40.002 --> 00:04:45.305
In the years that followed, the prominence and influence of Constantinople continued to decline, and the rise of

57
00:04:45.365 --> 00:04:48.788
Islam challenged the Byzantine Empire's political dominance.

58
00:04:49.664 --> 00:04:55.727
By 1393, the Ottoman Turks had conquered virtually all the land around the city and forced its residents

59
00:04:55.767 --> 00:04:56.467
to pay tribute.

60
00:04:57.207 --> 00:05:04.050
When Sultan Mehmed II finally conquered the city in 1453, he immediately went to the Hagia Sophia and

61
00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:05.151
declared it a mosque.

62
00:05:06.031 --> 00:05:10.553
Constantinople was now the capital of a third great realm—the Ottoman Empire.

63
00:05:11.054 --> 00:05:14.535
The sultan wanted the city to become a thriving center for culture and trade.

64
00:05:15.435 --> 00:05:19.626
Mehmed II began to repair the city and proclaimed freedom of religion.

65
00:05:20.206 --> 00:05:26.491
This encouraged merchants and artisans of all faiths to move back into the once-Christian capital, bringing with them

66
00:05:26.951 --> 00:05:30.033
trades such as ceramics, calligraphy, and bookbinding.

67
00:05:30.673 --> 00:05:34.716
Within fifty years, Constantinople was once more the largest city in Europe.

68
00:05:35.717 --> 00:05:42.961
Unfortunately, the city suffered again when an earthquake in 1509 destroyed nearly thirty kilometers of its seawalls, four

69
00:05:43.001 --> 00:05:44.963
thousand homes, and the treasury.

70
00:05:45.583 --> 00:05:51.945
Sultan Süleyman the Magnificent rebuilt the city, adding bridges, palaces, schools, and monuments.

71
00:05:52.286 --> 00:05:56.687
He also built some of the most glorious mosques in the city, including the Süleymaniye Mosque.

72
00:05:57.407 --> 00:06:01.848
The Ottoman Empire lasted about six hundred years and came to an end with World War I.

73
00:06:02.548 --> 00:06:09.430
After the war, its people, the Turks, fought a war for independence from the occupying Allied Powers.

74
00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:16.369
In 1923, they formed a new democratic country, the Republic of Turkey, under military leader and statesman Mustafa

75
00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:17.429
Kemal Atatürk.

76
00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:22.152
Atatürk declared that the city of Ankara would be the capital of Turkey.

77
00:06:22.353 --> 00:06:26.656
For the first time, Constantinople would not be a capital of any empire or country.

78
00:06:27.436 --> 00:06:31.379
Constantinople was officially renamed Istanbul in 1930.

79
00:06:32.339 --> 00:06:38.223
Today, Istanbul stands proudly on its own with an atmosphere incomparable to that of any other place in

80
00:06:38.264 --> 00:06:38.744
the world.

81
00:06:39.424 --> 00:06:45.180
With a population of over 13 million, the city is a dazzling mixture of past and present, East

82
00:06:45.281 --> 00:06:45.741
and West.

83
00:06:46.462 --> 00:06:52.248
To celebrate the diversity of its history and people, the European Union chose Istanbul as one of its

84
00:06:52.308 --> 00:06:55.110
"European Capitals of Culture" for 2010.

85
00:06:56.031 --> 00:07:01.397
Istanbul hopes to show the world that the modern city continues to uphold its legacy as a natural

86
00:07:01.477 --> 00:07:02.738
and dynamic crossroads.
